<p>HardPoint keeps your books in your own currency, and lets you take other currencies at the till and buy from suppliers who invoice in them.</p>

<h2>Setting rates</h2>
<p>Owners and managers add currencies in <strong>Settings › Currencies</strong>, with what one unit is worth in your currency (for example
  1 USD = 129.50 KES, or 1 UGX = 0.0348 KES). Update the rate when your bureau's or bank's changes. Each payment and order keeps the rate it used,
  so changing it never changes the past. Untick <strong>Take it at the till</strong> for currencies you only buy in.</p>

<h2>The daily market rate</h2>
<p>Each morning HardPoint fetches the market rate for every currency you use and shows it under your rate. <strong>Use …</strong> takes it
  as your rate once. Tick <strong>Follow the daily rate</strong> to have the rate updated every morning, less a margin you choose (for
  example 1.5%, so a dollar handed over is worth a little less than the market says, to cover the bank's charges and the rate moving before you
  bank it).</p>
<ul>
  <li>If the market rate moves more than 10% in a day, it isn't applied by itself. It's shown as <strong>Not applied</strong> until an owner or
    manager presses <strong>Use it</strong>, so a fault in the feed can't change what the till takes for a dollar.</li>
  <li>The market rate is a mid-market rate from a public feed, not a bureau's buying rate. Set the margin to match what you actually get.</li>
  <li>If the feed can't be reached, rates stay as they were.</li>
</ul>

<h2>At the till</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Choose <strong>Foreign</strong> as the payment method, pick the currency and type the notes handed over.</li>
  <li>The till shows what they're worth and the change, which is given in your currency. Notes worth less than what's due leave the rest to be
    paid another way.</li>
  <li>The receipt shows the notes and the rate.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Closing the till</h2>
<p>Count your own cash as usual, and each foreign currency separately. The Z report shows the foreign notes expected and counted, and the
  change given for them comes out of the expected cash.</p>

<h2>Suppliers in dollars</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Choose the supplier's currency when adding them (it can't change once there are orders or invoices).</li>
  <li>Their orders, invoices, payments and what you owe them are in that currency.</li>
  <li>When goods arrive, check the <strong>rate today</strong> on the receiving form: stock is costed in your currency at that rate, with transport
    and duty added in your currency.</li>
  <li>"What we owe" shows their balance in their currency, with the total in yours at today's rates; the VAT report converts their invoices at
    the rate recorded on each.</li>
</ul>
